## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to reason about data deletion in log-based, multi-tenant systems; covers tombstone propagation, privacy guarantees, auditability, and verification under failure conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Tombstone semantics, cross-topic propagation, and per-tenant isolation
- Idempotence, retention versus deletion semantics
- Audit trails and verification under failure conditions
- Handling cross-topic joins while preserving deletion markers
- Compression-aware deletion propagation

## Code Example
```javascript
function createTombstone(tenantId, key, deletionTimestamp) {
  return {
    tenantId,
    key,
    deletionTimestamp,
    isTombstone: true
  };
}

function propagateTombstone(tombstone, pipeline) {
  pipeline.forEach(stage => {
    stage.emit(tombstone);
    stage.compress(); // Preserve tombstone through compression
  });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates expertise in per-tenant event-time semantics, SLA-bound late data handling, and scalable state management in KCNA under multi-tenant workloads.

## Key Concepts

- Event-time processing with tenant-specific watermarks
- Per-tenant state stores and partitioning strategies
- Late data handling within SLA constraints
- Fault tolerance through checkpointing and recovery
- At-least-once delivery guarantees

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: per-tenant watermark advancement and late event routing
class TenantWindowManager {
  constructor() {
    this.watermark = -Infinity;
    this.windows = new Map();
  }

  onEvent(tenantId, event) {
    const isLate = event.timestamp < this.watermark;
    if (isLate) {
      this.routeToDeadLetterQueue(tenantId, event);
    } else {
      this.mergeIntoActiveWindow(tenantId, event);
    }
  }
}
```

